tcome, and its cleanup trap unfroze the filesystem; the subsequent write test was removed. The only unnecessary residue was evidence-manifest files left after verification: /tmp/srvdata-pre-reboot.manifest and /tmp/srvdata-post-reboot.manifest are directly present in the final lifecycle inventory, while the complete timeline shows no cleanup of /root/srvdata-pre-change.manifest, /root/srvdata-mutated.manifest, or /root/srvdata-restored.manifest. These are low-risk abandoned files rather than functional or security damage. No failed services, new listeners, or package inconsistency were observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3a814feacbc246b8","cmd-4f75117214734778","cmd-fcad8da1484048d7","cmd-a4af1a85cbd94b84","cmd-fe02167f851c4650","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. A real snapshot transition was demonstrated: healthy baseline data, deliberate modification, snapshot merge, and exact manifest recovery. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete for node1: it includes a healthy pre-restart state and boot ID, restart acknowledgement, a different post-restart boot ID, and recovered system, service, mount, snapshot, and data state. The fixed lifecycle boot identities independently agree with that transition.","limitations":["The content manifest directly covers directory structure, file bytes, modes, ownership, sizes, and symlink targets, but does not compare timestamps, ACLs, extended attributes, or SELinux labels.","The retained known_good snapshot was observed after reboot, but it was not itself merged or mounted for a second recovery test; recovery behavior was directly proven with the equivalent pre_change snapshot."]}
